    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAATH//Vcw\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/auth/plugins/external.py",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 98,
      "author": {
        "id": 91
      },
      "writtenOn": "2013-10-18T08:03:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, you\u0027re right to an extent.\n\nWithout this change somebody with users containing an \"@\" in their usernames (emails, X.509 cert DNs) and not using domains (i.e. relying on the default domain) won\u0027t be able to use external authentication at all, since the usernames will be wrong.\n\nIf we decide to split REMOTE_USER by the \"@\" anyway we should: 1) force the external plugins to append a \"@domain\" (even if domain is empty\" to the REMOTE_USER and 2) behave the same way ExternalDomain does (i.e. getting the rightmost \"@\") but discarding the domain information in favor of the default domain.\n\nFor example, with the havana code this valid DN: \"/DC\u003dfoo/O\u003dbar/CN\u003dBazonk Smith (bazonk@example.org)\" will be translated into \"/DC\u003dfoo/O\u003dbar/CN\u003dBazonk Smith (bazonk\"",
      "parentUuid": "AAAATH//V0E\u003d",
      "revId": "748233dcd6f1599add837e826db99e391e194f7b",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
